From: cvediver <cvediver@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 7 Sep 2026 12:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath12k: complete WSI group recovery on partial crashes

Group recovery after a firmware crash relies on every member of the WSI
group resetting: ath12k_core_reset() parks at the group barrier
("waiting for N partner device(s) to reset") until ag->num_started
reaches zero, and only then powers every member back up.  Three gaps
remain around that barrier on mixed AHB+PCIe groups (IPQ5332 +
2x QCN9274):

1. Only two paths ever queue reset work: a PCIe device's own MHI RDDM
   callback (self only) and the AHB rootPD SSR notifier (group-wide).
   A lone PCIe RDDM therefore tears down the whole group's mac state
   (ath12k_core_pre/post_reconfigure_recovery iterate every hw of the
   group) but resets only itself; the AHB member and a non-asserted
   PCIe partner stay attached and the barrier parks forever - all three
   radios down until reboot.  Queue reset work for every other
   registered, not-already-resetting group member when parking at the
   barrier, mirroring ath12k_ahb_queue_group_recovery() for every crash
   origin.

2. The group power-up loop discards the ath12k_hif_power_up() return
   value.  A userPD spawn/ready timeout right after a rootPD reload
   leaves that member down with no retry; since
   ath12k_core_hw_group_start_ready() then never becomes true, no hw of
   the group is ever restarted and mac80211 stays in_reconfig with
   queues stopped.  Retry the power-up once (with an intervening
   power-down) and log loudly if the member still fails.

3. ath12k_ahb_queue_group_recovery() sets ATH12K_FLAG_CRASH_FLUSH and
   ATH12K_FLAG_RECOVERY on every member before deciding whether to
   queue its reset.  A member skipped because it is already past its
   own reset (is_reset still set, restart in flight) may have already
   passed the only recovery-path clear site of CRASH_FLUSH in
   ath12k_core_qmi_firmware_ready(); the stale flag then permanently
   fails its WMI/CE paths with -ESHUTDOWN.  Set the flags only on
   members which are actually queued; a member already resetting
   manages its own flags.

A member mid-restart at fan-out time is still skipped and not reset
again (queueing it would self-cancel via the reset_count > 1 skip path
once its restart completes); back-to-back crashes within that window
remain unrecovered.

Signed-off-by: cvediver <cvediver@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/ahb.c  | 14 ++++--
 drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/core.c | 64 +++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 73 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/ahb.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/ahb.c
@@ -957,14 +957,20 @@
 		    !test_bit(ATH12K_FLAG_REGISTERED, &partner_ab->dev_flags) ||
 		    test_bit(ATH12K_FLAG_UNREGISTERING, &partner_ab->dev_flags))
 			continue;
-
-		set_bit(ATH12K_FLAG_CRASH_FLUSH, &partner_ab->dev_flags);
-		set_bit(ATH12K_FLAG_RECOVERY, &partner_ab->dev_flags);
 
+		/* A member that is already resetting manages its own flags.
+		 * Setting ATH12K_FLAG_CRASH_FLUSH on a member that already
+		 * passed its flag-clearing point in
+		 * ath12k_core_qmi_firmware_ready() would never be cleared
+		 * again and permanently kills its WMI/CE paths.
+		 */
 		if (READ_ONCE(partner_ab->is_reset) ||
 		    work_busy(&partner_ab->reset_work))
 			continue;
 
+		set_bit(ATH12K_FLAG_CRASH_FLUSH, &partner_ab->dev_flags);
+		set_bit(ATH12K_FLAG_RECOVERY, &partner_ab->dev_flags);
+
 		queue_work(partner_ab->workqueue_aux, &partner_ab->reset_work);
 	}
 	mutex_unlock(&ag->mutex);
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/core.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/core.c
@@ -1729,13 +1729,54 @@
 
 exit_restart:
 	complete(&ab->restart_completed);
+}
+
+/* Queue reset work for every other registered member of the WSI group.
+ *
+ * The group barrier in ath12k_core_reset() only releases once every
+ * member has powered down, but a member whose firmware did not crash on
+ * its own has no crash notification of its own: a lone PCIe RDDM leaves
+ * the AHB device (and a non-asserted PCIe partner) attached to the group
+ * and the barrier parked forever.  Mirror the rootPD fan-out from
+ * ath12k_ahb_queue_group_recovery() for every crash origin.
+ *
+ * Flags are only set on members which are actually queued here.  A
+ * member that is already resetting (or mid-restart, is_reset still set)
+ * manages its own flags; setting ATH12K_FLAG_CRASH_FLUSH on a member
+ * past its flag-clearing point in ath12k_core_qmi_firmware_ready()
+ * would never be cleared again and permanently kills its WMI/CE paths.
+ */
+static void ath12k_core_queue_partner_reset(struct ath12k_base *ab)
+{
+	struct ath12k_hw_group *ag = ab->ag;
+	struct ath12k_base *partner_ab;
+	int i;
+
+	lockdep_assert_held(&ag->mutex);
+
+	for (i = 0; i < ag->num_devices; i++) {
+		partner_ab = ag->ab[i];
+		if (!partner_ab || partner_ab == ab ||
+		    !test_bit(ATH12K_FLAG_REGISTERED, &partner_ab->dev_flags) ||
+		    test_bit(ATH12K_FLAG_UNREGISTERING, &partner_ab->dev_flags))
+			continue;
+
+		if (READ_ONCE(partner_ab->is_reset) ||
+		    work_busy(&partner_ab->reset_work))
+			continue;
+
+		set_bit(ATH12K_FLAG_CRASH_FLUSH, &partner_ab->dev_flags);
+		set_bit(ATH12K_FLAG_RECOVERY, &partner_ab->dev_flags);
+
+		queue_work(partner_ab->workqueue_aux, &partner_ab->reset_work);
+	}
 }
 
 static void ath12k_core_reset(struct work_struct *work)
 {
 	struct ath12k_base *ab = container_of(work, struct ath12k_base, reset_work);
 	struct ath12k_hw_group *ag = ab->ag;
-	int reset_count, fail_cont_count, i;
+	int reset_count, fail_cont_count, i, ret;
 	long time_left;
 
 	if (!(test_bit(ATH12K_FLAG_QMI_FW_READY_COMPLETE, &ab->dev_flags))) {
@@ -1804,6 +1845,7 @@
 	ath12k_core_to_group_ref_put(ab);
 
 	if (ag->num_started > 0) {
+		ath12k_core_queue_partner_reset(ab);
 		ath12k_dbg(ab, ATH12K_DBG_BOOT,
 			   "waiting for %d partner device(s) to reset\n",
 			   ag->num_started);
@@ -1819,7 +1861,27 @@
 		if (!ab)
 			continue;
 
-		ath12k_hif_power_up(ab);
+		ret = ath12k_hif_power_up(ab);
+		if (ret) {
+			/* One bounded retry: a userPD spawn or ready timeout
+			 * right after a rootPD reload can be transient, and a
+			 * member that stays down keeps
+			 * ath12k_core_hw_group_start_ready() false so no hw of
+			 * the group is ever restarted.
+			 */
+			ath12k_warn(ab,
+				    "failed to power up device during reset (%d), retrying\n",
+				    ret);
+			ath12k_hif_power_down(ab, false);
+			ret = ath12k_hif_power_up(ab);
+		}
+		if (ret) {
+			ath12k_err(ab,
+				   "failed to power up device during reset: %d; wireless stays down until the next crash or a reboot\n",
+				   ret);
+			continue;
+		}
+
 		ath12k_dbg(ab, ATH12K_DBG_BOOT, "reset started\n");
 	}
 
